<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benjamin Hornigold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1680–<a href="/years/1719">1719</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benjamin Hornigold was an English pirate based at Nassau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, one of the founders of the pirate community known as the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Pirates</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackbeard">Edward Teach</a>, later called Blackbeard, served under him. In 1718 he accepted a royal pardon from the new governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodes-rogers">Woodes Rogers</a> and turned pirate hunter. He died in a shipwreck in 1719.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hornigold's early life is unrecorded. Some claim he was born in the English county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, where the surname Hornigold or Hornagold appears; if so, he may have first served aboard ships from King's Lynn or Great Yarmouth. His first documented acts of piracy took place in the winter of 1713, when he used periaguas, which were sailing canoes, and the sloop Happy Return, alongside Daniel Stillwell, John Cockram and John West, to menace merchant vessels off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> and its capital Nassau, where he had established a privateers' or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>' republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hornigold himself sailed a ship named the Marianne. After a mutiny in the summer of 1716 he and his supporters were left with a captured sloop. By 1717 he commanded a thirty-gun sloop, the Ranger, probably the most heavily armed ship in the region, which let him seize other vessels with impunity. His second-in-command was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blackbeard">Edward Teach</a>, later known as Blackbeard, to whom he delegated his earlier sloop. In the spring of 1717 the two captains seized three merchant ships in quick succession: a Spanish ship carrying 120 barrels of flour bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, a Bermudian sloop with a cargo of spirits and a Portuguese ship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a> carrying white wine.</p><p>In September 1717 Hornigold and Teach met Major Stede Bonnet, who, wounded in battle, ceded command of his ship Revenge to Teach. Hornigold took care not to attack British-flagged ships, apparently to keep the legal defence that he was a privateer acting against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s enemies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. His lieutenants disliked this restraint, and the combined crews voted to attack any vessel they chose. Hornigold opposed the vote and was replaced as captain of the Marianne by Samuel Bellamy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hornigold accepted a royal pardon, sailing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> with the Ranger and another sloop to receive it from the governor there. He then became a pirate hunter for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodes-rogers">Woodes Rogers</a>, the new Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, who commissioned him to pursue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> who had refused to give up the trade, among them his former lieutenant Teach. In December 1718 Rogers wrote to the Board of Trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> commending his efforts to remedy his reputation.</p><p>In late 1719 Rogers commissioned Hornigold and other trusted captains to help trade with the Spanish. His ship was caught in a hurricane between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and wrecked on an uncharted reef. Captain Charles Johnson's A General History of the Pyrates records that five of his men escaped in a canoe. The location of the reef remains unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benjamin Hornigold” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Benjamin Hornigold</b> and Henry Jennings were leading figures, alongside Edward Teach, Charles Vane, Sam Bellamy and Calico Jack Rackham.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pirates">Republic of Pirates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.67,0.95&amp;year=1705" aria-label="Norfolk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Benjamin Hornigold"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5049 5625h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5049 5625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.67,0.95&amp;year=1705">What was here in 1705? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
